{{also|addlepate}}
==English==

===Etymology===
[[addle]] (''confuse, confused'') + [[pate]] (''head'')

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|idiom}} A foolish or dull-witted person.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1792|author=Thomas Holcroft|title=Anna St. Ives|passage=Am I to be ramshaklt out of the super nakullums in spite o' my teeth? Yea and go softly! I crack the nut and you eat the kernel!}}
#*: I tellee once again you've an addle pate o' your own! [...] Squire Nincompoop!
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1882|author=Frank Lee Benedict|title=Miss Alderly's Law-Suit|journal=Peterson's Magazine|volume=81-82|page=443
|passage=Then he executed a war-dance, and at its close stepped before Crawford, regarded him for an instant, and croaked out:<br>"Addle-pate! Addle-pate!"<br>"Upon my word, I believe you are the foul fiend himself," muttered Mark.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1910|author=Grace S. Richmond|title=Red Pepper Burns|passage=“Go to bed, and don't bother your '''addle pate''' about your neighbours. Can't a man cut up a few sticks without your coming to investigate?”}}

